            {
              "id": "glog-58444424-8218-4c60-aa7f-11f17c7a85c8",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `malloc` function in C++. `malloc` is a function that dynamically allocates memory at runtime. The problem arises when the size of the memory to be allocated is controlled by the user or an external input. This can lead to several types of vulnerabilities such as Buffer Overflow, Integer Overflow, or even Denial of Service (DoS) if the user requests more memory than the system can provide.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to:\n\n1. Always validate and sanitize user input. Never trust data from the user blindly.\n2. Limit the maximum amount of memory that can be allocated.\n3. Handle the case where `malloc` fails gracefully. If `malloc` fails, it returns a null pointer. Always check if the result of `malloc` is not null before using the allocated memory.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix for the vulnerability:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nif(args->sendBytes > MAX_ALLOWED_ALLOCATION) {\n    // Handle error\n} else {\n    char* buffer = (char*) malloc(args->sendBytes);\n    if(buffer == NULL) {\n        // Handle error\n    } else {\n        // Use buffer\n        free(buffer);\n    }\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, `MAX_ALLOWED_ALLOCATION` is a constant that defines the maximum amount of memory that can be allocated. This value should be chosen carefully, considering the resources of the system where the software will run.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `stdlib.h` library for the `malloc` function.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
