                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strlen` function in C and C++ is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to vulnerabilities if not used properly. The function counts the number of characters in a string until it reaches a null character (`\\0`). If the string is not properly null-terminated, `strlen` can continue reading past the intended boundary of the string, leading to a buffer overflow vulnerability. This can result in crashes, incorrect behavior, or even code execution.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, `strlen(\"DIV\")`, there is no direct vulnerability as the string \"DIV\" is a string literal which is automatically null-terminated by the compiler. However, if the string was taken from an untrusted source and not properly null-terminated, it could lead to the aforementioned issues.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always ensure that strings are properly null-terminated before passing them to `strlen`. If the string is taken from an untrusted source, consider using safer alternatives such as `strnlen`, which takes a maximum length parameter and will not read past this length, even if it does not encounter a null character.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe provided code snippet does not need fixing as it is not vulnerable. However, if the string was taken from an untrusted source, it could be fixed as follows:\n\n```c++\nchar* untrusted_source = /* ... */;\nsize_t max_length = /* ... */;\nsize_t length = strnlen(untrusted_source, max_length);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `strlen` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional libraries are needed to use it.\n\n## Resources\n\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to 