              "id": "glog-8db30d7e-2574-482a-a2a7-36a3874f4f10",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strlen` function in C and C++ is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to vulnerabilities if not used properly. The function counts the number of characters in a string until it reaches a null character (`\\0`). If the string is not properly null-terminated, `strlen` can continue reading past the intended boundary, leading to buffer over-read vulnerabilities. This can result in information disclosure or even a crash due to segmentation fault.\n\nIn your specific case, `strlen(\"OR\")` is not vulnerable because \"OR\" is a string literal which is automatically null-terminated by the compiler.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo avoid vulnerabilities related to `strlen`, follow these general guidelines:\n\n1. Always ensure that your strings are null-terminated.\n2. Consider using safer alternatives to `strlen` such as `strnlen` which takes a maximum length parameter and won't read past it.\n3. Be aware that `strlen` does not count the null character itself. If you use the result of `strlen` to allocate memory, remember to add 1 for the null character.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn your specific case, no fix is needed because `strlen(\"OR\")` is safe. However, if you were dealing with a non-null-terminated string, you could use `strnlen` as follows:\n\n```cpp\n#include <string.h>\n\nchar str[] = {'O', 'R'};\nsize_t len = strnlen(str, sizeof(str));\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `strlen` function is part of the C standard library, so you need to include `string.h` or `cstring` in C++.\n\n```cpp\n#include <string.h> // C\n#include <cstring> // C++\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerabilities](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-126: Buffer Over-read](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/126.html)"
