            {
              "id": "glog-2cef64d0-648a-480c-aa0d-26aef6c98310",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc` function in C is used to dynamically allocate memory at runtime. However, it can be a source of vulnerabilities if not used correctly. If the size argument passed to `malloc` is controlled by an attacker, it can lead to a buffer overflow or underflow, which can further lead to arbitrary code execution, denial of service, or information disclosure.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always validate and sanitize the input that is used to determine the size of the memory allocation. You should also check the return value of `malloc` to ensure that the memory allocation was successful before using the allocated memory.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix for the vulnerability:\n\n```c++\n// Assuming recvRegHandles is a pointer to some type\nif (recvRegHandles == NULL) {\n    // Handle error\n    return;\n}\n\n// Check for integer overflow\nif (SIZE_MAX / sizeof(*recvRegHandles) < num_elements) {\n    // Handle error\n    return;\n}\n\nrecvRegHandles = (type*) malloc(num_elements * sizeof(*recvRegHandles));\nif (recvRegHandles == NULL) {\n    // Handle error\n    return;\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, we first check if `recvRegHandles` is `NULL` to prevent dereferencing a `NULL` pointer. Then, we check for integer overflow when calculating the size of the memory allocation. Finally, we check the return value of `malloc` to ensure that the memory allocation was successful.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `stdlib.h` library for the `malloc` function.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerabilities](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)\n- [CWE-680: Integer Overflow to Buffer Overflow](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/680.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
