              "id": "glog-03b3d391-3d6d-46e1-a4d8-09c7f55a106d",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question pertains to the improper use of the `malloc` function in C++. `malloc` is a function that dynamically allocates memory at runtime. If not used correctly, it can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, memory leaks, and other unexpected behaviors. In this case, the vulnerability arises from the potential for an attacker to manipulate the size of the memory being allocated, leading to a buffer overflow.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to:\n\n1. Always validate the size of the memory being allocated. This can be done by checking the size parameter before calling `malloc`.\n\n2. Use functions that limit the amount of memory allocated, such as `calloc`.\n\n3. Always check the return value of `malloc`. If `malloc` fails, it returns a null pointer. Not checking for this can lead to null pointer dereferences.\n\n4. Free any memory that has been allocated once it is no longer needed to prevent memory leaks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix for the vulnerability:\n\n```cpp\nsize_t size = sizeof(RocdecSeiMessage);\nif (size > MAX_SIZE) {\n    // Handle error\n}\nRocdecSeiMessage* message = (RocdecSeiMessage*) malloc(size);\nif (message == NULL) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n// Use message\nfree(message);\n```\n\nIn this code, `MAX_SIZE` is a predefined constant that represents the maximum allowable size.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example does not require any specific library dependencies other than the standard C++ libraries.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/)\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerabilities](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)\n- [CWE-789: Uncontrolled Memory Allocation](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/789.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
